{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "At fixed interface_mobility and interfacial_tension, the target scales as dL_dt = interface_mobility*interfacial_tension*(1/L - L/L_max**2), so the whole trajectory is set by a single curvature-driven rate law with a time-invariant prefactor and finite-size suppression only through L.",
      "child_relation": "Release the assumption of time-invariant interface mobility by allowing the effective mobility to evolve during coarsening, so dL_dt keeps the curvature and finite-size structure but acquires history dependence through a state-dependent kinetic prefactor. The target then no longer depends on L alone among observed states.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "L",
        "interface_mobility"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "For trajectories with the same instantaneous L/L_max, the child can produce different dL_dt at early versus late times because the kinetic prefactor has evolved, whereas the parent predicts a unique rate once L and fixed parameters are specified."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "reduced_size",
        "expression": "L/L_max",
        "role": "distance from finite-size saturation where the parent suppression factor is assessed"
      },
      {
        "name": "curvature_drive",
        "expression": "1/L - L/L_max**2",
        "role": "net Gibbs-Thomson-like geometric driving factor already present in the parent"
      },
      {
        "name": "rate_per_tension",
        "expression": "L * (1/L - L/L_max**2)",
        "role": "checks whether departures from the parent 1/L-scaled law can be attributed to a changing kinetic prefactor rather than geometry alone"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when coarsening proceeds over enough time that interface kinetics age appreciably while 0 < L/L_max < 1 still spans a measurable range; strongest in the intermediate regime where finite-size arrest is not yet dominant but the microstructure has evolved beyond the initial transient.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "Within training trajectories, the same mechanism appears as a gradual deviation from the parent's one-to-one mapping between L/L_max and dL_dt. In holdout over longer times, the deviation extends to stronger late-time slowing at the same reduced size, revealing target-function history dependence rather than just a different constant prefactor.",
    "continuation_path": "If the mobility-relaxation timescale is sent to infinity or the mobility state is initialized and constrained to remain equal to the parent constant interface_mobility, the child reduces to the parent law exactly.",
    "freedom_change": "parameter_to_state",
    "promoted_symbol": "interface_mobility",
    "promotion_reason": "A fixed parameter cannot represent physically plausible kinetic aging of interface motion during the same quench experiment. To make the target depend on evolving interfacial kinetics rather than on L alone, interface_mobility must be released as a state.",
    "gap_statement": "The current model assumes the kinetic coefficient for interface motion is constant throughout coarsening, omitting possible time-dependent mobility relaxation of interfaces as defects anneal or local drag builds during the evolving microstructure.",
    "baseline_assumption": "Interface mobility and interfacial tension are constant.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Time-dependent effective interface mobility during curvature-driven coarsening, with geometry unchanged but kinetics gradually relaxing as the interface network coarsens.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "L",
      "interface_mobility",
      "L_max",
      "interfacial_tension"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the parent's fixed-environment shortcut that all kinetic resistance is frozen during the run. The parent target mapping is determined entirely by the current length scale L once the constants are chosen: for one shared parameter set, any observation with the same L/L_max must yield the same dL_dt. A scientifically natural child allows effective interface_mobility to evolve as a state, representing kinetic aging of interfaces during the same post-quench experiment while keeping interfacial_tension fixed. Then the scored target mapping changes, not merely an auxiliary trajectory: dL_dt at a given L can differ depending on the history of mobility evolution. The distinguishing condition is to compare earlier and later portions of the same admissible trajectory, holding interfacial_tension and L_max fixed and examining times for which the same reduced size L/L_max is visited across different runs or where nearby reduced sizes show systematic extra late-time slowing. The parent with one shared fixed interface_mobility cannot reproduce a history-dependent departure from the unique L-to-dL_dt relation; refitting the constant can only shift the whole trajectory uniformly, not create different effective slopes at equal reduced size across time. This refinement is observable when mobility evolution is not negligible over the measured coarsening window and before near-saturation suppression dominates. A limitation is that if kinetic aging is truly absent or too slow relative to the observation window, the promoted state would remain effectively constant and the parent law would suffice."
  },
